                    <span class="profile-eyebrow">A Life of Inner Discovery</span>
                    <h2>From an honest question to a light for countless seekers</h2>
                    <p>Bhaiyaji’s journey was not inherited as convention. It unfolded through inquiry, disciplined experience and complete surrender—until spiritual realization naturally expressed itself as service.</p>
                </header>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div>
                        <span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter I</span>
                        <h2>The question that changed a life</h2>
                    </div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>Born into the spiritually inclined family of Smt. Tulsi Devi and Shri Vijaykishan Ji Sharda, Nandkishore Sharda was an exceptional science student. In 1959, the passing of his father confronted the fifteen-year-old with the impermanence of the physical body.</p>
                        <p>That encounter awakened a deeper question: what is the eternal power that sustains life, and what is the true purpose of human existence? He resolved to discover the answer for himself. His search was guided by reason rather than blind acceptance; he looked beyond ritual, superstition and inherited explanations for knowledge that could withstand sincere inquiry.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div>
                        <span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter II</span>
                        <h2>Surrender, Sadhna and Divine Grace</h2>
                    </div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>With complete devotion, he dedicated himself to Jagatjanani Maa Tripurasundari. Years of intense Sadhna deepened his understanding of consciousness, the universe and the relationship between material and spiritual life.</p>
                        <p>Yet the knowledge he received was never treated as a private attainment. Bhaiyaji sought to express profound spiritual truths in a form that an ordinary person could understand, examine and practise while fulfilling family and social responsibilities. This became Buddhi-Vivek Yog Sadhna—a simple, contemporary path centred on awakened intelligence, discernment and inner steadiness.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <div class="profile-reflection">He sought truth through reason, sincerity and direct experience—and offered what he discovered for the welfare of all.</div>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div>
                        <span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter III</span>
                        <h2>When realization became service</h2>
                    </div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>In 1996, Bhaiyaji and Maa Basanti turned their spiritual conviction toward an urgent social need: the education of girls from disadvantaged families in western Rajasthan. The Swami Vivekanand Students’ Welfare Charitable Trust was established on 10 June 1996, with Maa Basanti as Founder and Bhaiyaji as President.</p>
                        <p>For them, education was incomplete without Sanskar. Financial assistance was therefore accompanied by character formation, confidence, health awareness, gratitude and a sense of responsibility. The Trust received the Rajasthan Government’s Bhamashah Award for five consecutive years from 1999 to 2003, recognition that Bhaiyaji regarded as belonging to the students who persevered against difficult circumstances.</p>
                        <p>On 24 January 2003, Vivekananda Jayanti, Bhaiyaji left his mortal form. His writings, the spiritual method he gave, the institutions he inspired and roads named in his honour in Jodhpur, Balotra and Jasol continue to carry his presence into public memory.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
